ning you're listening over and over  again you may be repeating I think  repeating out loud is a good thing to do  that's helping you catch the rhythm of  the language you're listening sometimes  to the language spoken slowly sometimes  to the language spoken more quickly for  long periods of time content that you  are listening to is not very clear like  it's it's you can't really pick out  where the words begin and end sometimes  you know the words but you can't make  out the whole meaning you just have a  vague sense of the meaning and gradually  everything starts to fall into place and  that process can take you six months to  a year I mean it can take four long run  even longer because there will always be  aspects of the language situations  movies conversations would be for  whatever that you continue not to  understand but the amount the the area  of the language that you're able to  understand that this starts to become  clear to you and natural to you gets  larger and larger and so you get a  natural feeling for the language and  your vocabulary increases and during  that period of getting acquainted with  the language I don't personally feel any  urgency to speak I have not sought out  people with whom to speak Russian which  is the language that I've been learning  recently obviously if I stumble on  someone who is a Russian speaker I will  try out my Russian it's fun I was even  in a situation in Holland after I had  only been listening to Russian about six  months for a blind Russian person in  this Dutch hotel was not being properly  looked after it was obviously in a state  of anxiety and I even with my limited  Russian was able to help him and make  him feel comfortable and tell him what  time he was supposed to get the bus and  so forth is the hotel staff were too  busy look after him properly and so I  was very satisfied I was able to use the  Russian  learn but I have no great urgency to  speak it and I'm not losing anything in  my opinion that's not to say that it  isn't a good thing I'm just going to  occasionally watch my time here I'm not  saying that it's not where is my time on  this thing anyways obviously it's a good  thing to speak whether it's a good thing  to speak speaking does a number of  things for you it forces you to try to  remember the things that you have been  learning passively and I think that  helps to prime the pump and then makes  you you know better able to remember  things it makes you conscious of where  your gaps are it makes you conscious of  things that you can't say words that you  don't have so that you start to look for  those in your listening and reading it's  a challenge so to the extent that you  meet the challenge it's satisfying to  the extent that you don't need the  challenge it can be sometimes a bit  discouraging sometimes it's a little bit  stressful it's a fun thing to do you  don't need a lot of it in my opinion if  after maybe a month or two of dedicated  listening a lot of listening to  repetitive listening to simple content  and you gradually start to move on to  more difficult content you have a bit of  vocabulary then you might want to speak  once a week that's enough if you're  fortunate enough to be in the country  where the language is spoken or you have  a friend then and if you have access to  more opportunities be fine go for it but  it's not crucial to me you need that  first period to build up the vocabulary  the familiar familiarity with the  language you know I often de-emphasize  grammar but obviously you've got to get  some sense of how the language works  initially by observation and there's  nothing wrong  being a small grammar book where you you  know just have a quick summary of it  don't try to remember any of the  explanations but just to get some sense  like okay learning Russian that you know  the past ends in an L and that the  feminine past tense is an L a so they  actually have a feminine form of the  past tense determined by the speaker so  you know these are a few little things  like that that when you get into the  more complex explanations the  conditionals the modal's the  subjunctives the the perfective verbs  and Russian all this kind of stuff  through the explanations for the  different cases is over this is no way  so let's get some sense of how the  language works and then start to observe  the language listen to the language like  the language you get the rhythm like the  music expand your vocabulary this can be  six to six months to a year or longer I  mean I have been a sense I have been  doing it for two years because I have no  real need to speak Russian I very much  enjoy my reading now that I'm reading  Russian history and reading Russian  literature of the 19th century I'm  listening to audiobooks of the same  material that's what I like to do I'm  happy doing what I'm doing with the  language I am meeting my objectives in  the language and at the same time I'm  I'm building myself up so that should I  go to Russia or you know not so long ago  I went to an evening of Russian theater  here in Vancouver and there was a lady  sitting beside me in order lady got to  be careful who I call older because I'm  not that young myself but I was able to  carry on a conversation with her in  Russian we talked about the play and we  talked about different things and that  that was a great source of satisfaction  so if it's there yes but I don't go out  looking for it and I think that we can  do a lot of good to ourselves if we  focus on this passive learning no  teacher correcting you no need to  perform no need to feel inadequate  nothing wrong with passive vocabulary it  hasn't yet converted itself into active  vocabulary so what I take great  satisfaction  in the fact that I've accumulated enough  vocabulary so that I can read you know  serious books good enough so and a dump  and this is the other thing I don't got  it careful it's got to be under ten  minutes here but all this business about  helping you read reading strategies pre  reading post reading I mean I got this  Russian textbook and they had all kinds  of like twelve or fifteen different  things to do before and after you read  no just read and listen enjoy it and  Reuben Alba's really stresses this and  he says somewhere he says there is  nothing as incompatible with the  enjoyment of reading as much as this  whole business of analyzing and  interpreting and and explaining what you  have just read the important thing is to  enjoy the music of a language to enjoy  the sounds of the language to enjoy the  the the wonders of literature  communicating with a person of a  different century of a different country  country a different culture in his  language and to get to that stage of  scriptures you can so silent period six  months one year eighteen months  depending on your circumstances it's a  great way to learn you shouldn't feel  under any obligation to speak and  therefore be wary of going to courses  where they force you to speak and thank  you goodbye for now
 

